 			<p>John was a mild man, but he was human; and after a long day’s work, to come home tired, hungry, and hopeful, to find a chaotic house, an empty table, and a cross wife was not exactly conducive to repose of mind or manner. He restrained himself, however, and the little squall would have blown over, but for one unlucky word.</p>
 			<p>“It’s a scrape, I acknowledge; but if you will lend a hand, we’ll pull through, and have a good time yet. Don’t cry, dear, but just exert yourself a bit, and knock us up something to eat. We’re both as hungry as hunters, so we shan’t mind what it is. Give us the cold meat, and bread and cheese; we won’t ask for jelly.”</p>
 			<p>He meant it for a good-natured joke; but that one word sealed his fate. Meg thought it was <em>too</em> cruel to hint about her sad failure, and the last atom of patience vanished as he spoke.</p>
